                "title": "Apply the power rule",
                "text": "$$4^{\\frac{x+1}{2}}=\\left(2^2\\right)^{\\frac{x+1}{2}}=2^{\\frac{2\\left(x+1\\right)}{2}}=2^{x+1}$$",
                "variabilization": {},
                "oer": "",
                "license": ""
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "id": "aeba74cA222-lawsexpsamebase-P12a-h3",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "aeba74cA222-lawsexpsamebase-P12a-h2"
        ],
        "title": "Apply power rule",
        "text": "Simplify expression using laws of exponents \\n $$\\log_{2}\\left(\\left(2^2\\right)^{\\frac{x+1}{2}}\\right)=\\log_{2}\\left(2^{\\frac{2\\left(x+1\\right)}{2}}\\right)=\\log_{2}\\left(2^{x+1}\\right)$$",
        "variabilization": {},
        "oer": "",
        "license": ""
    },
    {
        "id": "aeba74cA222-lawsexpsamebase-P12a-h4",
        "type": "hint",
        "dependencies": [
            "aeba74cA222-lawsexpsamebase-P12a-h3"
        ],
        "title": "Logs undo exponents",
        "text": "Logs reverse what an exponential does.\\n\\n That means if you take the log base $$2$$ of an exponential with base $$2$$, you get back the power (the exponent).\\n $$\\log_{2}\\left(2^{x+1}\\right)=x+1$$",
